WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.581
Least privilege applies to developers, operators, applications, pipelines, service identities, and support roles.

2
00:00:07.581 --> 00:00:11.606
Not everyone who can deploy code needs to read every secret.

3
00:00:11.606 --> 00:00:15.699
Not every runtime identity needs access to every integration.

4
00:00:15.699 --> 00:00:19.791
Narrow access makes mistakes and incidents easier to contain.

5
00:00:19.791 --> 00:00:23.414
Separation of duties can help for high-impact secrets.

6
00:00:23.414 --> 00:00:31.397
Creating, reading, rotating, approving, and deleting secrets may involve different roles depending on the organization.

7
00:00:31.397 --> 00:00:40.857
The goal is not bureaucracy for its own sake; the goal is to prevent one unchecked path from controlling sensitive access without visibility.

8
00:00:40.857 --> 00:00:50.316
Access reviews should cover secret stores, deployment systems, CI/CD variables, runtime identities, support tools, and administrative groups.

9
00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:56.555
People change teams, applications retire, integrations move, and emergency access can linger.

10
00:00:56.555 --> 00:01:00.245
Periodic review keeps access aligned with current need.

11
00:01:00.245 --> 00:01:02.056
Audit trails are essential.

12
00:01:02.056 --> 00:01:11.583
Reads, writes, rotations, permission changes, failed access attempts, and emergency actions should be recorded where the platform supports it.

13
00:01:11.583 --> 00:01:22.183
Audits help operators answer what changed, who accessed a value, which identity performed a rotation, and whether a suspected exposure needs broader response.

14
00:01:22.183 --> 00:01:27.013
Monitoring should look for unusual access patterns at a defensive level.

15
00:01:27.013 --> 00:01:37.948
Unexpected reads, access from unusual identities, repeated failures, sudden permission changes, or use outside normal deployment windows may warrant investigation.

16
00:01:37.948 --> 00:01:42.376
Monitoring supports response; it does not replace least privilege.

17
00:01:42.376 --> 00:01:46.871
Incident readiness should be prepared before exposure is suspected.

18
00:01:46.871 --> 00:01:56.599
A response plan should cover containment, rotation, revocation, redeployment, downstream notification, operational monitoring, and communication.

19
00:01:56.599 --> 00:02:04.985
Afterward, teams should improve inventory, ownership, automation, and review so unmanaged secrets do not become normal again.
